\section{LESSON SNAPSHOT}
\section{Students}
In this lesson, students follow their teacher in reading through a series of increasingly difficult explanations of the question about leaf changes.

Goals

Student Materials

\begin{itemize}
  \item Reflecting on the process of writing explanations

  \item Using text structure to convey information

  \item Writing syntactically and semantically complex phrases and clauses

  \item Copy of the text of the selected texts

  \item Paper

  \item Pens Procedure

\end{itemize}

Task 1: The teacher thinks aloud and asks questions as she reads each text.

Task 2: The teacher explains the text structure that makes the information easier to understand.

Task 3: The students draft a synthesis paragraph of the explanations by combining separate ideas into one big sentence.

Task 4: Students revise their sentences so that each change that happens has a reason.

\section{Teacher Modeling}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Writing "How and Why" Questions

  \item Using Text-Based Frames to Scaffold Writing

\end{itemize}

\section{Section 2: Sample Unit with Lessons}
\section{BACKGROUND INFORMATION}
\section{Purpose}
This lesson engages students in reading and writing explanations. This is a critical form of writing for academic content areas because content focuses on the reasons why things are the way they are: The reasons why the Civil War began or why our leaves change color here. Writing explanations requires the syntactic and grammatical features of noun phrases, prepositional phrases, appositive phrases, adjectival clauses, and conjunctions to name only a few.

The purpose of this lesson is to engage students in reading-writing-speaking as they analyze and draft a synthesis paragraph from complex content. This is a writing sample that demonstrates task performance at a much more condensed level.

